# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hume-dialogues-3.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1 has "cp → many_as_one"; hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1.2 and hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1.3 have "pagan_nogain → many_as_one"; they differ when only "nothing is gained by the Pagan hypothesis that the deities arose from the unknown powers of nature, since it is as easy to suppose all men and animals sprang immediately from a like origin" is true, which the first allows
hume-dialogues-3.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1.2 and hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "nothing is gained by the Pagan hypothesis that the deities arose from the unknown powers of nature, since it is as easy to suppose all men and animals sprang immediately from a like origin" is true and "Cleanthes' principles are sound" and "a numerous society of deities is as explicable as one universal deity who possesses within himself the powers and perfections of the whole society" are false. Is S2, "Push the same inference a step further, and you will find a numerous society of deities as explicable as one universal deity, who possesses within himself the powers and perfections of the whole society.", true then?
hume-dialogues-3.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1 and hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1.2 have nothing from it; hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1.3 has "many_as_one → like_footing"; they differ when only "a numerous society of deities is as explicable as one universal deity who possesses within himself the powers and perfections of the whole society" is true, which the first allows
hume-dialogues-3.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered yes, which keeps hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1 and hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "a numerous society of deities is as explicable as one universal deity who possesses within himself the powers and perfections of the whole society" is true and "the systems of Scepticism, Polytheism and Theism are on a like footing, and no one of them has any advantage over the others" is false. Is S3, "All these systems, then, of Scepticism, Polytheism, and Theism, you must allow, on your principles, to be on a like footing, and that no one of them has any advantage over the others.", true then?
hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1 has "pagan_nogain ∧ many_as_one → like_footing" and "¬like_footing"; hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1.2 has "many_as_one → like_footing" and "¬like_footing"; they differ when only "a numerous society of deities is as explicable as one universal deity who possesses within himself the powers and perfections of the whole society" is true, which the first allows; hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1.3 has "¬like_footing"
hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1.2 and hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if a numerous society of deities is as explicable as one universal deity who possesses within himself the powers and perfections of the whole society, then the systems of Scepticism, Polytheism and Theism are on a like footing, and no one of them has any advantage over the others"; hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1 does not
hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1.2 and hume-dialogues-3.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if a numerous society of deities is as explicable as one universal deity who possesses within himself the powers and perfections of the whole society, then the systems of Scepticism, Polytheism and Theism are on a like footing, and no one of them has any advantage over the others"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
